LD50 FOR TWINKIES ESTABLISHED

(AP-WASHINGTON) The Food and Drug Administration today has released
toxicity test results on the commercial food product generally known as
Twinkies.

Toxicity is typically expressed in milligrams per kilogram of body mass,
and is called LD50.  The LD50 is determined by giving a population of
test animals controlled amounts of a tested drug.  When fifty percent of
the test population dies at a given dose, the standard of toxicity is
established.

"Animal rights groups were vehemently opposed to feeding rats or monkeys
Twinkies," said an unnamed FDA official.  "We used adolescent midwest
boys instead.  No one seemed to mind.  And of course, we had to alter
the scaling for the Twinkies tests, just as we did for Dorito and the
infamous Cherry Hump tests.  Instead of milligram per kilogram of body
mass, we used grams per kilogram.  We were appalled at the dangerous
toxicity of this flavorful treat."

The LD50 for Twinkies has been established at 120-145 grams per kilogram
of body weight.  For a typical 160 pound midwest adolescent, this would
mean if ten of them each ate between 19.2 pounds and 23.2 pounds of
Twinkies, half of them would suffer a lethal fatality, or possibly just
die.

"The others would be okay, though," the FDA spokesman said.  "We don't
want to be alarmist about this threat.  And of course, if they let us
test with monkeys, it would only have been a couple dozen Twinkies.
Way, way less than that if we'd gone with the rats.  But think about
it.  Twenty pounds of Twinkies is only maybe a case and a half.  Hell,
my own kid can eat half that after school, and still have room for
dinner an hour later.  The Twinkie threat is real."

A press release from Hostess disputed the FDA findings.  "Some of these
adolescents lived well into their eighties before they died, and it
isn't clear that they died of Twinkie-related symptoms.  We don't think
these fatalities should be legitimately included in the FDA's LD50
results."  A Hostess spokesperson indicated that the company will be
filling a formal complaint regarding the Twinkie results.  "What's
next?" the spokesperson said, "Ho-Ho's?  Those disgusting marshmallow
bunnies?  Fruitcakes?"

"Yes," said the unnamed FDA official.

